provider_responses.text:
The main issue is not whether the team wants an extreme outcome. It is whether the project can be designed to produce reliable results without breaking the team, the tools, or the workflow.

The team has identified an old process that keeps reactivating under pressure. Their goal is to make a new process so dominant that, in real operating conditions, the old one no longer affects decisions or performance.

The risk is that the team treats this as a demolition project:

> “Destroy the old system completely.”

That is not a workable engineering standard. A better standard is:

> “Ensure the old system cannot take control under the conditions that matter.”

To do that, the team needs to:

- define the failure condition precisely;
- build one clear replacement procedure;
- rehearse it repeatedly;
- test it under realistic pressure;
- reduce the importance and influence of the old procedure;
- measure whether the new procedure activates faster and more reliably.

But there is a second problem: the team may be tempted to pursue maximum intensity—constant drills, no recovery, escalating pressure, and trying to eliminate every sign of the old system immediately.

That can turn the project into:

> overload → breakdown → restart → overload again.

A difficult project succeeds through sustained execution, not through forcing the system beyond its capacity every day.

So the practical objective is not perfection in theory. It is dependable control in operation:

- when the trigger occurs, the team follows the new protocol;
- under stress, the protocol still works;
- setbacks are treated as data, not proof the project has failed;
- recovery and pacing protect the team’s ability to continue;
- progress is measured by behavior and performance, not by an impossible demand for total internal absence.

In short: build a stronger operating system, test it under pressure, and pursue durability rather than self-destructive intensity.

